146
00:17:01.371 --> 00:17:02.412
<v Maya>Oh, this is the best part.</v>

147
00:17:02.892 --> 00:17:09.475
<v Daniel>Despite all this high-level math and careful negotiation, not everyone actually got their physical book at the end of the experiment.</v>

148
00:17:09.615 --> 00:17:11.856
<v Maya>A complete market failure at the very last mile.</v>

149
00:17:11.936 --> 00:17:18.259
<v Daniel>Nobody enforced the physical delivery. People just forgot to bring the books to the office where they couldn't find the co-worker they traded with.</v>

150
00:17:18.763 --> 00:17:33.378
<v Maya>And it perfectly highlights a critical concept explored in our final background paper, an economy of AI agents. A functioning market is not just about matching supply and demand or finding perfect mathematical preference alignment. A market</v>

151
00:17:33.458 --> 00:17:34.920
<v Maya>requires institutions.</v>

152
00:17:35.248 --> 00:17:36.349
<v Daniel>meaning the rules of the road.</v>

153
00:17:36.449 --> 00:17:50.915
<v Maya>Precisely. If an AI agent breaks a contract who is legally liable, how do you verify the identity of the agent you are negotiating with? Is the bot representing a real person, a corporation, or is it just a scam designed to drain resources?</v>

154
00:17:50.975 --> 00:17:53.876
<v Daniel>Yeah, privacy versus observability is a huge issue.</v>

155
00:17:54.037 --> 00:18:01.400
<v Maya>Exactly. Project Swap's failed deliveries prove that without institutional enforcement, the digital math means absolutely nothing.</v>

156
00:18:01.873 --> 00:18:15.128
<v Daniel>The authors of Project Swap actually propose some concrete solutions for these future institutions. They suggest we might need to treat AI agents with fiduciary duty, making them legally bound to act in our best financial interest, just like a human financial advisor.</v>

157
00:18:15.188 --> 00:18:16.369
<v Maya>Which would be huge.</v>

158
00:18:16.649 --> 00:18:31.221
<v Daniel>They even float the idea of licensing tests for bots. Imagine an AI having to pass a digital version of the Series 65 exam complete with, like, know your customer rules and a show me sample decisions prototype test</v>

159
00:18:31.581 --> 00:18:33.883
<v Daniel>before it is legally allowed to trade on your behalf.</v>

160
00:18:34.103 --> 00:18:46.671
<v Maya>They also discussed the necessity of mandatory replay logs. If a deal goes sideways, the human must have legal recourse to read exactly what their agent said and did. Oh, and agent IDs or personhood credentials.</v>

161
00:18:46.831 --> 00:18:52.955
<v Daniel>Right. To verify who is who. And another major proposal is strict rate limits on how often agents can message each other.</v>

162
00:18:53.155 --> 00:19:07.870
<v Maya>Right. To stop spamming, but also to solve the congestion problem. Going back to that cohesion singularity paper, they predicted that if negotiating costs dropped to zero, agents will just endlessly haggle over pennies, clogging up the entire economy. Rate limits force the agents to</v>

163
00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:08.991
<v Maya>actually make a decision.</v>

164
00:19:09.191 --> 00:19:23.904
<v Daniel>To be fair to the Anthropic team, they are very transparent about the limitations of their own study. The employees who built Claude are probably unusually trusting of it compared to the general public. There were no real financial incentives to rank the books perfectly. Only 59% of the</v>

165
00:19:23.944 --> 00:19:33.753
<v Daniel>participants even bothered to answer the final survey. And as we noted, the market rules were completely fixed, with zero adversarial bots trying to break the system.</v>

166
00:19:33.873 --> 00:19:47.323
<v Maya>But even factoring in those limitations, the synthesis of what we've learned today is a vital preview of the near future. As we rapidly move toward a world where agents negotiate for us, the hardest technical challenge is not the haggling.</v>

167
00:19:47.564 --> 00:19:48.444
<v Daniel>It's the understanding.</v>

168
00:19:48.544 --> 00:20:03.459
<v Maya>Exactly. The true bottleneck is representation, the struggle of a machine to truly understand human desire. Furthermore, the future of market inequality will very likely be dictated by who can afford to license the most capable AI. The</v>

169
00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:06.963
<v Maya>smartest agent sees the board better and wins the best price.</v>

170
00:20:07.143 --> 00:20:21.779
<v Daniel>Which leaves you with something very real to chew on. Think about the friction in your own daily life, applying for jobs, dealing with health care billing, browsing for a house. If you are going to eventually let an AI agent search and bargain for your livelihood, what kind of</v>

171
00:20:21.859 --> 00:20:36.722
<v Daniel>test or license should a market require of that agent before it is allowed to speak your name? Is the friction of doing it yourself actually a protective barrier against being misunderstood? Grounded in reality, without the sci-fi hype and without the doom,</v>

172
00:20:37.063 --> 00:20:39.547
<v Daniel>that is a question we are all going to have to answer very, very soon.</v>
